.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_REALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_realloc_entry \- reallocate an entry to a different size
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

void *ccol_r_mempool_realloc_entry(ccol_r_mempool *rmp, void *addr, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Changes the size of an allocated entry. The original pointer comes
back unchanged, and nothing is copied, when a move could not give the
entry more bytes than it already holds. That happens in two cases:
when the entry already holds exactly what the tier for the new size
would give it, or when it already holds exactly the number of bytes
the caller asked for. In every other case a new entry is allocated for
the request, min(old_size, new_size) bytes are copied into it, and the
old entry is freed.
.fi
.PP
Both of those questions are about the bytes that the entry holds, so a
pool-owned entry and a dynamic fallback entry that hold the same number of
bytes behave the same way here.
.PP
How many bytes an entry holds does depend on where it came from, and that is
the only difference the two representations force. A pool-owned entry holds the
whole element size of its tier, and so does a dynamic fallback entry that a
tier handed out after it ran out of its own slots. An entry from the shared
fallback that ccol_fallback_at_last_exhaustion uses holds exactly the size it
was created for.
.PP
If the function can't allocate a new entry, it returns the entry unmoved when
the request still fits inside it, and reports no failure. This is the same for
both kinds of entry, since a shrink does not fail for want of memory that it
doesn't need. The pointer that comes back then addresses at least the bytes
that the caller asked for, as on every other path that succeeds.
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp   Ranged memory pool
addr  Existing entry to reallocate, or NULL to allocate a new one
size  New size in bytes (must be > 0 and <= largest_size)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the reallocated entry on success
Returns NULL on failure, and then addr stays valid, does not move and
stays with the caller, as with realloc(3). That holds both for an
invalid size and for a valid size that the pool cannot serve
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
With a NULL addr the call is the same as ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry()
The original pointer comes back unchanged, and nothing is copied,
  when the entry already holds as many bytes as a move could give it
In every other case a new entry is allocated, the data is copied and
  the old entry is freed. If that allocation fails, the function
  returns addr unmoved, not NULL, when the request still fits inside
  the bytes that addr already holds
It copies min(old_user_size, new_user_size) bytes
It frees the old entry when the reallocation succeeds
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded=false
It returns NULL if size is 0 or above largest_size, and it does NOT
  touch addr: when size itself is invalid, an addr that is not NULL is
  neither freed nor moved, so the state is the same as if you had
  never made the call
It asserts if addr is not NULL and did not come from this
  ccol_r_mempool. That is the check for corruption and for a foreign
  pointer, the same one that ccol_r_mempool_free_entry() makes
It asserts if rmp is NULL
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_free_entry (3)
